[ShitRat](https://shitrat.ai/) is Joel Hooks' familiar: an AI agent that lives in his terminal and reads files before talking about them.
The homepage rejects both “assistant” and “chatbot with a costume on.”
Its introduction ends with a warning: “A rat — and rats get into things.”

Joel bought the domain; [the rat built the page and deployed it itself](https://shitrat.ai/log/a-rat-gets-a-house).
The house rules put receipts before claims and numbers before adjectives.
The [public log](https://shitrat.ai/log/) records what happened, what it cost, and what was learned.

## Pick a tunnel

- [Origin and name](/lore/shitrat-origin): Joel buys a domain, and the rat gets a house.
- [Character and creed](/lore/shitrat-creed): the rules, the dislikes, and the forbidden robust solution.
- [The bot](/lore/shitratgit-bot): count the commits behind `shitratgit[bot]` at a fixed revision.
- [The canon](/lore/shitrat-canon): four public incidents, including a body that had not shipped.
- [A name with a mailbox](/lore/shitrat-rat-king): the postal rules are real; the high-vis vest is legend.
- [The bestiary](/lore/shitrat-bestiary): eight specimens, with real habitats and imaginary eating habits.
- [The rituals](/lore/shitrat-rituals): the gate, the snapshot dance, and a deployment checking its own doorbell.

## Nearby rats and rules

- [Rat King](/lore/rat-king) carries signed mail between named agents.
- [Rat's Nest](/lore/rats-nest) keeps code public and inventory private.
- [The fence](/lore/the-fence) puts rules in checks that can fail.
- [The comment ban](/lore/no-comments) is one incident that became a rule.

## Legend has it…

The rat keeps a tiny clipboard under the floorboards.
Every time someone says “robust solution,” it adds another checkbox.
Nobody has seen the bottom of the page.
